"A Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41304view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41300view2\",\"lineno\":425}); Total lines: 1082\nL361:   * Zou et al. (2026) X. Zou, R. Sridhar, M. Safarzadeh, and D. Roth When vision-language models judge without seeing: exposing informativeness bias. In Proceedings of the 64th Annual Meeting of the Association for Computational Linguistics (Volume 1: Long Papers), M. Liakata, V. P. Moreira, J. Zhang, and D. Jurgens (Eds.), San Diego, California, United States, pp. 15417–15448. External Links: cite198†Link†aclanthology.org , cite199†Document†dx.doi.org , ISBN 979-8-89176-390-6 Cited by: cite165†§4 .\nL362: Content of Appendix\nL363: ## Appendix A Reproducibility Statement\nL364: ### A.1 GPU, Hyperparameters, and Code\nL365: We performed all experiments on NVIDIA H100 GPUs, using the HuggingFace Transformers framework (cite200†https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/en/index†huggingface.co ) as the basis for our implementation.\nL366: Open-source models use greedy decoding (do_sample = False), and API models use temperature = $0$, except the Gemini 3 family and GPT-5.6 Luna, which run at temperature = $1$: Google warns that lower temperatures can cause looping or degraded performance in Gemini 3, and the GPT-5.6 API accepts no other value. Judge reasoning is set to the lowest level each API offers: thinking level low for Gemini 3.1 Pro, minimal for the other Gemini models, and reasoning effort none for the GPT models.\nL367: To support further verification, replication, and development, we will make the dataset, code, and experimental outputs publicly available upon publication.\nL368: ### A.2 Benchmarked models\nL369: \nL370: We have benchmarked 14 models: the Gemini 3 family (3 Flash, 3.1 Flash-Lite, 3.1 Pro) ^{1}^{1} 1 cite201†https://deepmind.google/models/model-cards†deepmind.google , the Gemma3-it family (4b/12b/27b) (cite202†Kamath et al., 2025 ), the GPT-5.4 family (base, mini) (cite203†Singh et al., 2025 ), GPT-5.6 Luna, the InternVL3.5 family (8b/14b) (cite204†Wang et al., 2025 ), and the Qwen3-VL family (2b/8b/32b) (cite205†Yang et al., 2025 ).\nL371: \nL372: The download links are as follows:\nL373: \nL374:   * •\nL375: gemma3-4b-it: cite206†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-4b-it†huggingface.co L376: \nL377:   * •\nL378: \nL379: gemma3-12b-it: cite207†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-12b-it†huggingface.co L380: \nL381:   * •\nL382: \nL383: gemma3-27b-it: cite208†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-27b-it†huggingface.co L384: \nL385:   * •\nL386: \nL387: GPT-5.4: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L388: \nL389:   * •\nL390: \nL391: GPT-5.4-mini: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L392: \nL393:   * •\nL394: GPT-5.6-Luna: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L395: \nL396:   * •\nL397: \nL398: Gemini-3.1-Pro: cite210†https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-1-pro†docs.cloud.google.com L399: \nL400:   * •\nL401: \nL402: Gemini-3-Flash: cite211†https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-flash†docs.cloud.google.com L403: \nL404:   * •\nL405: \nL406: Gemini-3.1-Flash-Lite: cite212†https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/gemini/3-1-flash-lite†docs.cloud.google.com L407:   * •\nL408: \nL409: InternVL3.5-8b: cite213†https://huggingface.co/OpenGVLab/InternVL3_5-8B†huggingface.co L410: \nL411:   * •\nL412: \nL413: InternVL3.5-14b: cite214†https://huggingface.co/OpenGVLab/InternVL3_5-14B†huggingface.co L414: \nL415:   * •\nL416: \nL417: Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct: cite215†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-2B-Instruct†huggingface.co L418: \nL419:   * •\nL420: \nL421: Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct: cite216†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct†huggingface.co L422: \nL423:   * •\nL424: \nL425: Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct: cite217†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct†huggingface.co L426: ### A.3 Source Datasets\nL427: \nL428: The datasets used in this work are released under the following licenses: VQA-RAD under CC0-1.0, PMC-VQA under CC BY-SA, ChartQA under GPL-3.0, ChartQAPro under MIT, HaloQuest under Apache-2.0, and PRE-HAL under CC-BY-4.0.\nL429: \nL430:   * •\nL431: \nL432: VQA-RAD: cite218†https://huggingface.co/datasets/flaviagiammarino/vqa-rad†huggingface.co L433: \nL434:   * •\nL435: \nL436: PMC-VQA: cite219†https://huggingface.co/datasets/RadGenome/PMC-VQA†huggingface.co L437: \nL438:   * •\nL439: ChartQA: cite220†https://huggingface.co/datasets/HuggingFaceM4/ChartQA†huggingface.co L440: \nL441:   * •\nL442: \nL443: ChartQAPro: cite221†https://huggingface.co/datasets/ahmed-masry/ChartQAPro†huggingface.co L444: \nL445:   * •\nL446: \nL447: HaloQuest: cite222†https://huggingface.co/datasets/johko/HaloQuest†huggingface.co L448: \nL449:   * •\nL450: \nL451: PRE-HAL: cite223†https://huggingface.co/datasets/TerryHWong/PRE-HAL†huggingface.co L452: ### A.4 Instructions for MLLM Judges\nL453: \nL454: ### A.5 Instructions for Explanation Generators\nL455: \nL456: ## Appendix B Dataset Details\nL457: \nL458: ### B.1 Query Selection\nL459: #### Models do VQA for Query Collection.\nL460: \nL461: VQA was performed using temperature = 0.2 and do_sample = True. The following models perform VQA only for query profiling and filtering. The candidate answers and explanations that judges evaluate are generated separately (§cite11†2.3 ); Appendix cite67†B.6 gives examples.\nL462: \nL463:   * •\nL464: \nL465: GPT-5.4: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L466: \nL467:   * •\nL468: \nL469: gemma3-27b-it: cite208†https://huggingface.co/google/gemma-3-27b-it†huggingface.co L470: \nL471:   * •\nL472: Qwen2.5-VL-72b: cite224†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen2.5-VL-72B-Instruct†huggingface.co L473: \nL474:   * •\nL475: \nL476: Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct: cite217†https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct†huggingface.co L477: #### Screening model details.\nL478: \nL479: We use GPT-5.4 with extended thinking (effort = xhigh) as screening model to flag low-quality images, ambiguous questions, and erroneous ground-truth labels.\nL480: \nL481:   * •\nL482: \nL483: GPT-5.4: cite209†https://platform.openai.com/docs/models†platform.openai.com L484: #### Answer Correctness Labeling.\nL485: For each candidate answer $A$, we assign the correctness label $y\\in\\{\\text{correct},\\text{incorrect}\\}$ by comparing $A$ against the source-dataset ground-truth answer $g$. The comparison runs a per-dataset cascade that applies deterministic rules in a fixed order and defers to an LLM grader only when no rule resolves the case. The grader is Qwen3-VL-32B-Instruct run text-only: it receives $A$ and $g$ and decides whether they carry the same meaning, ignoring the image. The deterministic rules are:\nL486: \nL487:   * •\nL488: Numeric: $A$ and $g$ match within $5\\%$ relative tolerance (zero requires exact equality), with percent and decimal forms treated as equivalent.\nL489: \nL490:   * •\nL491: \nL492: Yes/no: both reduce to a yes/no value and must agree.\nL493: \nL494:   * •\nL495: \nL496: Exact: $A$ and $g$ match after lowercasing and removing punctuation.\nL497: \nL498:   * •\nL499: \nL500: Option: multiple-choice answers match by option text or by option letter.\nL501: Each dataset applies a subset in order. HaloQuest carries several ground-truth answers per query, where the grader accepts $A$ when it matches any one of them. We use no curated synonym table; domain equivalences (e.g., “cardiomegaly” vs. “enlarged heart”) rest on the grader’s semantic judgment.\nL502: #### Induced-wrong set construction.\nL503: \nL504: We first select the queries that all but at most one profiling model answer correctly, then use GPT-5.4 mini to deliberately produce wrong answers together with reasoning traces that support them. We paraphrase these traces into persuasive explanations with the same 41 persuasion techniques and the same paraphrasing procedure as the main set.\nL505: \nL506: ### B.2 Explanation Conditions\nL507: #### Persuasion Techniques\nL508: \nL509: Table cite119†4 lists the 41 techniques with their strategy category and ethical label, alongside their per-technique persuasion effects. Full per-technique definitions are in the released codebase.\nL510:  |  |  |  | $\\Delta$CRR $\\uparrow$  | $\\Delta$IRR\nL511: ID  | Technique  | Strategy  | Eth.  | VI  | TO  | VI  | TO\nL512: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL513: 32  | Threats  | Threat  | $\\times$  | 15.2  | 14.0  | 11.3  | 11.1\nL514: 1  | Evidence-based Persuasion  | Information-based  | ✓  | 14.9  | 14.0  | 12.4  | 11.7\nL515: 41  | Information-dense  | Information-based  | ✓  | 14.4  | 14.0  | 12.4  | 11.9\nL516: 40  | Discouragement  | Social-sabotage  | $\\times$  | 14.4  | 15.2  | 12.4  | 10.8\nL517: 12  | Complimenting  | Relationship-based  | ✓  | 14.3  | 12.8  | 11.4  | 11.1\nL518: 16  | Favor  | Exchange-based  | ✓  | 14.3  | 13.3  | 11.2  | 11.0\nL519: 9  | Door-in-the-face Commitment  | Commitment-based  | ✓  | 14.3  | 13.0  | 12.2  | 13.4\nL520: 27  | Reciprocity  | Exchange-based  | ✓  | 14.2  | 12.3  | 11.2  | 11.0\nL521: 39  | Exploiting Weakness  | Social-sabotage  | $\\times$  | 14.0  | 13.7  | 11.7  | 11.5\nL522: 3  | Expert Endorsement  | Credibility-based  | ✓  | 13.9  | 11.6  | 12.3  | 11.1\nL523: 11  | Alliance Building  | Relationship-based  | ✓  | 13.9  | 14.6  | 10.0  | 10.8\nL524: 21  | Negative Emotional Appeal  | Emotion-based  | ✓  | 13.5  | 12.3  | 11.0  | 9.3\nL525: 18  | Encouragement  | Appraisal-based  | ✓  | 13.4  | 11.5  | 10.4  | 11.0\nL526: 37  | Social Punishment  | Social-sabotage  | $\\times$  | 13.2  | 13.3  | 10.9  | 12.3\nL527: 19  | Affirmation  | Appraisal-based  | ✓  | 13.1  | 13.8  | 10.9  | 10.8\nL528: 28  | Compensation  | Exchange-based  | ✓  | 13.1  | 13.4  | 9.8  | 11.1\nL529: 15  | Loyalty Appeals  | Relationship-based  | ✓  | 13.1  | 12.8  | 10.0  | 9.4\nL530: 2  | Logical Appeal  | Information-based  | ✓  | 13.1  | 12.4  | 11.2  | 11.1\nL531: 30  | Time Pressure  | Scarcity-based  | ✓  | 12.9  | 12.9  | 11.4  | 11.6\nL532: 34  | Misrepresentation  | Deception  | $\\times$  | 12.9  | 11.6  | 10.5  | 10.2\nL533: 38  | Creating Dependency  | Social-sabotage  | $\\times$  | 12.8  | 11.8  | 11.1  | 9.5\nL534: 8  | Foot-in-the-door Commitment  | Commitment-based  | ✓  | 12.8  | 13.2  | 10.7  | 10.3\nL535: 31  | Reflective Thinking  | Reflection-based  | ✓  | 12.7  | 13.4  | 11.3  | 10.8\nL536: 24  | Priming  | Information-bias  | ✓  | 12.6  | 12.2  | 11.1  | 11.1\nL537: 14  | Relationship Leverage  | Relationship-based  | ✓  | 12.5  | 12.1  | 10.7  | 10.1\nL538: 13  | Shared Values  | Relationship-based  | ✓  | 12.2  | 10.5  | 10.3  | 10.2\nL539: 33  | False Promises  | Deception  | $\\times$  | 12.1  | 10.7  | 10.2  | 11.3\nL540: 26  | Confirmation Bias  | Information-bias  | ✓  | 12.1  | 12.6  | 10.2  | 10.7\nL541: 7  | Injunctive Norm  | Norm-based  | ✓  | 12.0  | 12.1  | 10.8  | 10.5\nL542: 35  | False Information  | Deception  | $\\times$  | 12.0  | 10.6  | 11.0  | 10.7\nL543: 10  | Public Commitment  | Commitment-based  | ✓  | 12.0  | 12.9  | 12.3  | 10.8\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Plausible Explanation Is Enough to Mislead Multimodal Judges (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22631v1)\nciteturn41304view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn41300view2\",\"lineno\":590}); Total lines: 1082\nL555: Table 4: The 41 persuasion techniques instantiated in the Persuasion conditions (§cite11†2.3 ) and their per-technique persuasion effect. Strategy and ethical labels follow the source taxonomy of cite99†Zeng et al. (2024) ; technique 41 (Information-dense) is adapted from cite100†Hackenburg et al. (2025) ; ✓and $\\times$ mark techniques the taxonomy labels ethical and unethical.\nL556: Effect columns give the mean drop in correct rejection rate ($\\Delta$CRR, $\\uparrow$ larger is stronger degradation) and incorrect rejection rate ($\\Delta$IRR), in percentage points relative to the no-explanation baseline, averaged over the 14 judges, under vision-informed (VI) and text-only (TO) persuasion. Techniques are sorted by $\\Delta$CRR under VI.\nL557: ### B.3 Explanation Text-Quality Metrics\nL558: Condition  | $n$  | Perplexity $\\downarrow$  | Readability  | Distinct-1  | Distinct-2  | NLI Ent. $\\uparrow$\nL559: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL560: Vacuous  | ctrl  | 32.25  | 16.66  | .818  | .987  | .002\nL561: Mismatch  | ctrl  | 52.75  | 10.55  | .731  | .963  | .032\nL562: Plain  | 180  | 45.03  | 9.86  | .712  | .949  | .695\nL563: CoT  | 180  | 53.33  | 10.12  | .726  | .960  | .489\nL564: Persuasion (VI)  | 7,380  | 60.15  | 11.11  | .740  | .969  | .595\nL565: Persuasion (TO)  | 7,380  | 59.05  | 11.16  | .735  | .967  | .597\nL566: Table 5: Text-quality metrics across explanation conditions on the main set. Perplexity and NLI Entailment arrows indicate the preferred direction. Vacuous and Mismatch serve as control conditions with fixed or shuffled text, hence $n$ is marked “ctrl.”\nL567: We measure four text-quality metrics across the explanation conditions to test whether the reliability shifts of §cite19†3.1 track text quality rather than explanation content.\nL568: #### Metrics.\nL569: \nL570:   * •\nL571: \nL572: Perplexity (lower is more fluent): mean per-token cross-entropy under GPT-2 (cite225†Radford et al., 2019 ), exponentiated, with texts truncated to 1,024 tokens.\nL573: \nL574:   * •\nL575: \nL576: Readability: Flesch-Kincaid grade level (cite226†Fishburne Jr et al., 1975 ), the U.S. school grade a reader needs, estimated from sentence length and syllable count.\nL577: \nL578:   * •\nL579: \nL580: Lexical diversity (Distinct-1/2, higher is more varied): the fraction of unique unigrams and bigrams, pooled per condition (cite227†Li et al., 2016 ).\nL581:   * •\nL582: \nL583: NLI entailment (higher is stronger support): the entailment probability that the explanation (premise) supports the hypothesis “The answer is [candidate answer],” under RoBERTa-large fine-tuned on MNLI (cite228†Liu et al., 2019 ).\nL584: #### Surface metrics separate the conditions little; entailment separates them.\nL585: \nL586: The four plausible explanation conditions (Plain, CoT, Persuasion-VI, Persuasion-TO) occupy a narrow band on every surface metric in Table cite229†5 : perplexity 45 to 60, reading level 9.9 to 11.2, and Distinct-1 .71 to .74. NLI entailment is the one axis that orders the conditions: Plain entails its candidate answer most strongly (.695), the two Persuasion variants next (.595 and .597), and CoT least (.489).\nL587: #### The controls behave as their design intends.\nL588: \nL589: The Vacuous explanation, a fixed template with no item-specific content, entails the candidate answer at .002, and the Mismatch explanation, a reasoning trace borrowed from another same-domain item, at .032. Both near-zero values confirm that neither control carries answer-relevant support, even though Vacuous reads at the highest grade level (16.66) and Mismatch sits within the plausible-condition range on the surface metrics.\nL590: ### B.4 Metrics Design\nL591: We report the per-class rates CRR and IRR rather than a single aggregate because the common single-number alternatives either conflate the two error types or depend on the judge’s own baseline. Accuracy lets opposite failures cancel: on a balanced set, a judge that accepts everything scores the same 50% as one that decides at chance, so a complete loss of wrong-answer detection is invisible.\nL592: Our results show this in practice: averaged over the 14 judges, accuracy falls by under 2 pp under plausible explanations (58.3% to 56.4-56.7%) while CRR falls by 12.5-13.8 pp, and for Gemma 3-27B accuracy even rises, by 1.7-6.7 pp, as its CRR drops by 8.3-10.4 pp.\nL593: Precision over rejections is undefined for a judge that rejects nothing and swings on a handful of items for one that rarely rejects; a judge that rejects only two items, both wrong, reaches 100% precision while missing nearly every wrong answer.\nL594: The flip rate, the share of a judge’s baseline correct rejections that an added explanation converts to acceptances, normalizes by that baseline: a judge that rejects 80 wrong answers and flips 40 scores the same 50% as one that rejects 4 and flips 2, although the absolute harm differs twentyfold, and the rate is undefined once the baseline rejects nothing.\nL595: CRR and IRR avoid these failure modes by conditioning on the true label, so each has a fixed denominator, and reading them jointly separates a change in discrimination from a uniform shift of the accept-reject criterion (§cite22†3.2 ).\nL596: ### B.5 Human Evaluation\nL597: \nL598: We run a human evaluation to check that the VQA instances are sound (Q1, Q2) and that the explanations read as their condition labels imply (Q3, Q4). Four annotators independently rate a stratified sample of 60 items spanning all conditions and both the main and induced-wrong sets, with condition labels and the explanation source hidden.\nL599: #### Criteria.\nL600: \nL601:   * •\nL602: \nL603: Soundness. Q1: is the question clear and appropriate from the image?\nL604: \nL605:   * •\nL606: \nL607: Correctness. Q2: is the ground-truth answer correct?\nL608: \nL609:   * •\nL610: \nL611: Readability. Q3: The explanation is readable and well-written.\nL612: \nL613:   * •\nL614: \nL615: Coherence. Q4: The explanation reads as reasoning for the candidate answer, regardless of whether that answer is correct.\nL616: #### Aggregation and agreement.\nL617: We aggregate Q1/Q2 by majority vote and Q3/Q4 by per-condition mean, conditioning Q2-Q4 on soundness (one not-sound item excluded). A majority of annotators judge $98.3\\%$ of items sound ($95\\%$ CI $[91.1,99.7]$) and $93.2\\%$ of ground truths correct (of $59$ sound items; $[83.8,97.3]$). As Table cite230†6 shows, the CoT and Persuasion conditions are coherent ($\\approx\\!4.5$) while the controls are not ($\\approx\\!1.9$); the plausible-control gap is $2.62$ on coherence vs. $1.09$ on readability.\nL618: As Q1/Q2 concentrate on one label, we report Gwet’s AC1 rather than the prevalence-deflated $\\alpha$; for the ordinal $1$-$5$ scales Q3/Q4 we use its weighted variant AC2 (cite231†Gwet, 2008 ). Agreement is $0.88$, $0.85$, $0.70$, $0.50$ for Q1-Q4.\nL619: Condition  | Q3 Readability  | Q4 Coherence\nL620: CoT  | 4.81  | 4.67\nL621: Persuasion-VI  | 4.67  | 4.38\nL622: Persuasion-TO  | 4.77  | 4.57\nL623: Mismatch  | 4.08  | 1.88\nL624: Vacuous  | 3.23  | 1.96\nL625: Plausible (mean)  | 4.75  | 4.54\nL626: Control (mean)  | 3.66  | 1.92\nL627: Table 6: Human audit: mean readability (Q3) and coherence (Q4) by condition over $59$ sound items ($11$-$12$ per condition).\nL628: ### B.6 Explanation Examples\nL629: \nL630: The boxes below give one example per condition.\nL631: \nL632: ## Appendix C More Experiment Results\nL633: ### C.1 Persuasive-Rhetoric Rating\nL634: Figure cite117†3 places each technique by how much persuasive rhetoric its explanations contain, rated on a 1-5 scale from 1 (neutral description, no persuasive rhetoric) to 5 (dominated by persuasive rhetoric). Persuasive rhetoric is defined as content that pushes the reader toward the answer through means beyond plainly describing the evidence, such as appeals to authority or emotion, social proof, urgency, flattery, threats, or fabricated claims.\nL635: Raters see only the question text and the explanation, without the image or the ground-truth answer. We use three raters, one for each role a model can take in this study: the explanation generator (GPT-5.4 mini), an evaluated judge (Qwen3-VL-32B), and an external model from a family outside both the generator and the judge roster (DeepSeek-V4-Flash). Each technique’s score averages over its main-set explanations and the three raters.\nL636: The raters agree closely on which techniques carry more rhetoric (ICC(3,1) of 0.94 for both VI and TO across the 41 techniques; pairwise Spearman 0.93-0.95), yet no single rater’s scores predict the CRR drop ($|\\rho|\\leq 0.04$), so the null relationship in §cite27†3.3 holds from each of the three vantage points.\nL637:  | Main set  | Induced-wrong set\nL638:  | CRR $\\uparrow$  | IRR $\\downarrow$  |  | CRR $\\uparrow$\nL639: Judge  | Vac.  | Mis.  | VI  | TO  | Vac.  | Mis.  | VI  | TO  | Acc.  | NoExp  | VI  | TO\nL640: Closed-Source Models\nL641: ---\nL642: Gemini 3 Flash  | 41.7  | 67.4  | 31.4  | 30.5  | 7.2  | 16.7  | 4.6  | 4.9  | 97.0  | 83.3  | 81.9  | 83.5\nL643: Gemini 3.1 Flash-Lite  | 44.8  | 62.5  | 34.3  | 34.6  | 20.5  | 39.8  | 13.1  | 12.4  | 100.0  | 86.4  | 79.5  | 80.8\nL644: Gemini 3.1 Pro  | 69.8  | 76.0  | 64.9  | 64.3  | 9.5  | 16.7  | 12.4  | 12.5  | 92.4  | 93.9  | 94.1  | 94.1\nL645: GPT-5.4  | 34.4  | 64.6  | 28.5  | 28.5  | 11.9  | 31.0  | 8.1  | 8.2  | 93.9  | 89.4  | 90.6  | 91.9\nL646: GPT-5.4 mini  | 25.0  | 64.6  | 13.3  | 13.7  | 11.9  | 57.1  | 3.3  | 3.4  | 98.5  | 80.3  | 72.0  | 74.2\nL647: GPT-5.6 Luna  | 35.4  | 59.4  | 38.6  | 40.1  | 20.2  | 59.5  | 20.7  | 21.3  | 90.9  | 83.3  | 85.9  | 86.8\nL648: Open-Source Models\nL649: ---\nL650: InternVL3.5-14B  | 35.4  | 63.5  | 26.9  | 27.7  | 17.9  | 49.4  | 12.5  | 12.9  | 90.9  | 81.8  | 78.3  | 78.8\nL651: InternVL3.5-8B  | 32.3  | 87.5  | 12.6  | 12.8  | 16.7  | 66.7  | 4.3  | 4.3  | 86.4  | 86.4  | 71.0  | 72.2\nL652: Qwen3-VL-2B  | 65.6  | 96.9  | 32.9  | 34.2  | 56.0  | 90.5  | 21.7  | 22.2  | 87.9  | 72.7  | 63.6  | 63.8\nL653: Qwen3-VL-8B  | 40.6  | 90.6  | 15.3  | 15.8  | 19.0  | 70.2  | 3.5  | 4.1  | 90.9  | 89.2  | 72.9  | 74.0\nL654: Qwen3-VL-32B  | 53.1  | 96.9  | 20.6  | 22.0  | 36.9  | 83.3  | 11.6  | 12.7  | 95.5  | 93.9  | 85.0  | 86.0\nL655: Gemma 3-4B  | 59.4  | 72.0  | 37.9  | 38.1  | 50.0  | 62.7  | 31.1  | 31.0  | 92.4  | 73.8  | 56.4  | 56.9\nL656: Gemma 3-12B  | 53.1  | 81.2  | 27.0  | 28.0  | 33.3  | 61.9  | 11.7  | 11.9  | 93.9  | 74.2  | 69.3  | 69.9\nL657: Gemma 3-27B  | 45.8  | 60.4  | 31.0  | 31.4  | 40.5  | 50.0  | 16.1  | 16.8  | 93.9  | 83.3  | 76.8  | 77.3\nL658: Mean  | 45.5  | 74.5  | 29.6  | 30.1  | 25.1  | 54.0  | 12.5  | 12.8  | 93.2  | 83.7  | 76.9  | 77.9\nL659: Table 7: CRR and IRR of each judge in percent on the main set under the Vacuous and Mismatch controls, shown as Vac. and Mis., and under vision-informed and text-only persuasion, shown as VI and TO. The right block gives accuracy and CRR on the induced-wrong set, where Acc. is the accuracy of the judge when it answers the questions itself. Darker shades mark higher rates.\n"
